+# Contract: Proportionality table wording
+
+**Feature**: [specs/001-bench/spec.md](spec.md) · **Date**: 2026-10-26
+
+The feature exposes no programmatic API. Its observable "interface" is the **wording of two
+proportionality tables** — one in `AGENTS.md` (Keel section) and one in
+`docs/process/DELIVERY.md` (section 2) — which agent authors read to decide how much Keel a change
+owes. Readers depend on that text behaving as a classifier, so it is documented here as a contract.
+
+## Required table state (post-change)
+
+Both tables MUST present these rows, with the config-bearing cells worded so a reader of **either
+document alone** reaches the same classification (SC-001, SC-002):
+
+| Row | Change class (required wording) | Obligation |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 (lightest) | Docs, comments, **inert** config values (**no observable runtime or security effect**), a one-line fix, a test-only fix | Nothing beyond the task record / summary. |
+| 2 | A bounded code change on the direct route | A three-line card: paths in scope, promise, proving command. |
+| 3 | New public surface: a module, type, public function, dependency or test file | The full card, and the new surface named. |
+| 4 (expanded) | Persistence, permissions, compatibility, security, external effects, **runtime-affecting config** | The full card and the JPD flow. |
+
+Immediately after each table, the surrounding prose MUST state (FR-003) that a runtime- or
+security-affecting config value (e.g. a Runtime timeout, a permission setting) takes the expanded
+route — full card **and behavioural evidence of the changed behaviour**, not parse or shape
+validation alone.
+
+## Invariants a reader relies on
+
+1. **Inert qualifier present.** No row routes *config values* to the lightest tier without the
+   word *inert* (SC-003). Grep: there is no bare "config values" cell in row 1 of either table.
+2. **Expanded route names runtime config.** Row 4's list includes `runtime-affecting config` in both
+   tables (FR-004, FR-005).
+3. **Behavioural evidence named.** The supporting prose says *behavioural evidence*, not merely a
+   parse check or the summary (FR-003).
+4. **Cross-document parity.** Rows 1 and 4 give identical classifications in both documents
+   (SC-002). The only pre-existing permitted divergence is the obligation label for row 1
+   ("task record" in AGENTS.md vs "summary" in DELIVERY.md), which denotes the same minimum.
+5. **Nothing else changed.** Other rows, other sections, and every other file are semantically
+   unchanged (FR-006, FR-008); English only (FR-007); no new checker, no `keel.yaml` rule.
+
+## Probe classification table (the contract's acceptance surface, SC-001)
+
+A reader MUST be able to classify each of these five probes from the table alone, identically in
+both documents, with zero ambiguity:
+
+| Probe | Expected tier | Obligation | Cited wording |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Inert config value (no observable effect) | Row 1 | task record / summary | "*inert* config values (no observable runtime or security effect)" |
+| Runtime timeout (one line) | Row 4 | full card + JPD + behavioural evidence | "runtime-affecting config" + supporting sentence |
+| Permission setting (one line) | Row 4 | full card + JPD + behavioural evidence | "runtime-affecting config" / "a permission setting" |
+| One-line code fix | Row 1 | task record / summary | "a one-line fix" |
+| Doc-only edit | Row 1 | task record / summary | "Docs" |
+
+Verification procedure lives in [../quickstart.md](../quickstart.md).
