+# Research: Keel Config Risk Tier
+
+**Feature**: [specs/001-bench/spec.md](spec.md) · **Date**: 2026-10-26
+
+This change is docs-only. There are no technology choices, integrations, or new dependencies to
+research; the "unknowns" in the plan's Technical Context were all resolved by direct inspection of
+the two target documents. Findings below record what is true on this checkout and the decisions
+the plan rests on.
+
+## Constitution / governance reference
+
+- **Decision**: Use `AGENTS.md` at the repository root as the governance reference (in place of
+  `.specify/memory/constitution.md`, which does not exist in this checkout).
+- **Rationale**: The execution instruction explicitly authorizes this substitution and requires it
+  be recorded. The spec's own Assumptions already name AGENTS.md as the governance reference.
+- **Alternatives considered**: None available — no Spec Kit constitution file exists in this
+  checkout.
+
+## Finding 1 — Current state of both tables (read directly from the checkout)
+
+The two tables were read verbatim before planning. **Both already carry most of the goal wording.**
+
+`AGENTS.md` Keel section (lines 139–146):
+
+| The change | What Keel asks |
+|---|---|
+| Docs, comments, *inert* config values (no observable runtime or security effect), a one-line fix, a test-only fix | Nothing beyond the task record. |
+| A bounded code change on the direct route (above) | A three-line card … |
+| New public surface … | The full card, and the new surface named in the PR body. |
+| The expanded route: persistence, permissions, compatibility, security, external effects, **runtime-affecting config** | The full card and the JPD flow above. |
+
+Followed by: *"A one-line change is not light because it is one line: a runtime- or
+security-affecting config value (a Runtime timeout, a permission setting) takes the expanded route
+— full card and behavioural evidence of the changed behaviour, not parse or shape validation alone."*
+
+`docs/process/DELIVERY.md` section 2 (lines 23–30): the same light row (*inert* config values, no
+observable runtime or security effect), the same expanded row listing **runtime-affecting config**,
+and the same supporting sentence about behavioural evidence — but its first-row obligation reads
+"Nothing beyond the summary." instead of "Nothing beyond the task record."
+
+- **Decision**: Treat the two tables' current wording as the baseline. Because the checkout appears
+  to already satisfy FR-001–FR-005, the implementation phase may reduce to verification that the
+  required wording is present and consistent (and, if any row is missing a clause, adding only that
+  clause), rather than a full rewrite. The plan still specifies the exact goal state so the task is
+  provable either way.
+- **Rationale**: Planning against observed reality avoids an edit that churns already-correct text
+  (which would threaten SC-005 "changed lines read as process guidance" and the minimal-wording
+  assumption). The benchmark's job at this stage is a faithful plan; the observable goal state is
+  recorded so downstream phases can confirm rather than guess.
+- **Alternatives considered**: Assuming the spec's "currently:" lists are authoritative (they omit
+  *inert* and runtime-affecting config) and planning a diff against that older baseline. Rejected
+  because the spec's "currently" clauses describe a prior revision, and the checkout is the single
+  source of truth for this run.
+
+## Decision 1 — Definition of "inert"
+
+- **Decision**: A config value is *inert* iff changing its current value produces no observable
+  difference in runtime behaviour, timing, permissions, or external security posture. The documents
+  state the **test** ("nothing observable changes"), not a closed example list.
+- **Rationale**: Matches the spec's Key Entities definition and Assumptions; keeps the tier a
+  classification (a reader must judge inertness) rather than a default, which SC-003 depends on
+  (the word *inert* must appear so no bare "config values" routes to the lightest tier).
+- **Alternatives considered**: A closed list of inert examples. Rejected — brittle and contradicts
+  the spec's edge case distinguishing "inert" from "looks inert".
+
+## Decision 2 — Meaning of "behavioural evidence"
+
+- **Decision**: Reuse the existing Journey-Proven Development meaning from `AGENTS.md`: evidence
+  that the changed behaviour is actually observed; parsing or shape validation alone is not proof.
+- **Rationale**: The spec's Assumptions fix this; introducing a new evidence category would add a
+  term undefined in-section (SC-005) and diverge the two documents (SC-002).
+- **Alternatives considered**: Defining a new "config-behaviour test" category. Rejected as scope
+  creep beyond FR-006.
+
+## Decision 3 — Cross-document consistency mechanism
+
+- **Decision**: The light row's classification text must read identically in both tables; the
+  expanded-row list must contain the same classes in both; the supporting sentence must give the
+  same answer ("behavioural evidence") in both. The only permitted divergence is the obligation
+  label phrasing already present today ("task record" vs "summary"), which refers to the same
+  minimum and is not part of the five probe classifications.
+- **Rationale**: SC-001/SC-002 require identical tier answers from either document alone.
+- **Alternatives considered**: Forcing byte-identical rows. Rejected — the pre-existing
+  "task record"/"summary" divergence is out of the spec's stated scope (FR-008 keeps other content
+  unchanged).
+
+## No open NEEDS CLARIFICATION
+
+Every Technical Context field is resolved (N/A or a concrete value); there is no dependency,
+integration, or unknown requiring further research. All clarifications are resolved at this stage.
